<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John II Komnenos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1087">1087</a>–<a href="/years/1143">1143</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as John II Comnenus · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komnenian-dynasty">Komnenian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John II Komnenos was Byzantine emperor from 1118 to 1143, the son of Alexios I. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, Hungarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and recovered lands from the Turks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In 1137 to 1138 he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, forcing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> to accept his overlordship and besieging Shaizar with Frankish allies. Admired for his justice and piety, he died after a hunting accident.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos.jpg" alt="John II Komnenos" width="320" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_II_Comnene.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was born on 13 September 1087, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> and Irene Doukaina, and so had the status of porphyrogennetos. He was crowned co-emperor as a child, in 1092. His accession was contested: Irene favoured Nikephoros Bryennios, husband of her eldest child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-komnene">Anna Komnene</a>, and Anna herself is held by most historians to have aspired to the throne. As Alexios lay dying on 15 August 1118, John took the imperial signet ring from him with the help of his brother Isaac, rode to the Great Palace with armed followers, and was acclaimed emperor. Anna was later kept from society for thirty years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Because of the family intrigues, John appointed men from outside the imperial family to high office, unlike his father. His brother Isaac, though given the title of sebastokrator, plotted against him and fled in 1130 to the Danishmend emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>; he was exiled in 1139 to Heraclea Pontica for the rest of John's life. In the west John kept an alliance with the German emperors against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. His strategy relied on taking and holding fortified places, and he conducted about twenty-five sieges himself.</p><p>In 1119–1121 he defeated the Seljuq Turks in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and in 1122 he crushed a Pecheneg invasion at the Battle of Beroia, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangian-guard">Varangian Guard</a> broke the wagon laager. In 1124–1126 a quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, whose 1082 trading treaty he had refused to confirm, ended when he reconfirmed it. He also fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in the late 1120s. In 1137 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adana">Adana</a> and Mopsuestia from Armenian Cilicia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-of-poitiers">Raymond of Poitiers</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joscelin-ii-of-edessa">Joscelin II of Edessa</a> acknowledged him as overlord. He then led a joint campaign into Muslim Syria, which failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. He also built the Monastery of Christ Pantokrator in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, with a hospital of five wards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1142 John campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> under direct rule; his eldest son Alexios died of a fever during the campaign. While hunting wild boar on Mount Taurus he cut his hand on a poisoned arrow, and he died on 8 April 1143, probably of septicaemia. The suggestion of assassination has little support in the sources. His youngest son Manuel I succeeded him. Historians such as George Ostrogorsky and John Birkenmeier regard his reign as among the most successful of the Komnenian period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John II Komnenos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emirs paid tribute or fought as needed against the Principality of Antioch, the Nizari Ismailis and Turkish rulers of Aleppo, and withstood a siege by the Byzantine emperor <b>John II Komnenos</b> and Frankish allies in 1138.</blockquote><a href="/state/munqidhite-emirate-of-shaizar">Munqidhite Emirate of Shaizar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1123" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John II Komnenos"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1123">What was here in 1123? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
